  "query_preview": "../Monday through Thursday/...\nAn active weather pattern will persist across SEAK through next \nweek. The big player that will send the disturbances into the Gulf\nand through SEAK will be the remnants of Typhoon Dujuan and \nassociated upper- level low/trough that will slowly swing through \nsouthern interior of AK and into the Gulf through early next week.\nAdditionally, in between each front, cooler, moist onshore flow \nwill keep mostly cloudy skies and showers. Sunday night into \nMonday, a low/front will will pivot around the periphery of the \nparent upper-low and lift northeast into the Gulf and strengthen \nas it approaches the panhandle, with another surge of gusty winds.\nThis system will tap into a deep feed of Pacific moisture, with \nmoderate to heavy rain expected. In addition, cooler temperatures \naloft associated with the upper trough will result in modest \ninstability, bringing a potential for isolated thunderstorms, \nprimarily along the Gulf Coast and southern panhandle. For Tuesday\ninto Wednesday, rain chances and precip rates are favored to be \non a downward trend as the primary Gulf low weakens and the feed \nof deeper moisture shifts southeast of the panhandle towards BC. \nContinuous showers are still expected through Wednesday afternoon \nbefore an upper level trough slides down and replaces the dying \nlow over the Eastern Gulf. This new disturbance will help steer \nmore moisture into the panhandle for Thursday and into the \nextended range. The rainy and cloudy pattern will keep \ntemperatures seasonably cool with highs generally in the low to \nmid 50s.",
